ill=20
cease manufacturing the Model S sedan and Model X SUV=E2=80=94the flagship =
vehicles=20
that launched the electric revolution=E2=80=94to repurpose factory space fo=
r Optimus=20
robot production lines expected to begin operating before the end of 2026.

 The third-generation Optimus, designed specifically for mass manufacturing=
,=20
will be revealed in Q1 2026 with significantly upgraded hands and dexterity=
=20
capabilities that surpass the current V2.5 units already performing basic=
=20
battery-handling tasks in Tesla factories. Musk's target is staggering: 1=
=20
million robots per year at production capacity, with consumer sales potenti=
ally=20
beginning in 2027 at an estimated price of $20,000-$30,000.

=20
<https://www.theverge.com/transportation/869746/tesla-optimus-gen-3-q1-2026=
-earnings>
The implications for Tesla's valuation are existential in either direction.=
=20
Musk has repeatedly stated that Optimus "will be worth more than everything=
=20
else at Tesla combined"=E2=80=94more than the car business, more than energ=
y, more than=20
Full Self-Driving=E2=80=94and his $1 trillion compensation package literall=
y requires=20
production of at least 1 million units to vest fully. If Optimus succeeds a=
s a=20
general-purpose domestic helper, factory worker, and eventually surgical=20
assistant as Musk envisions, the addressable market dwarfs the global=20
automobile industry.

 But the execution challenges are enormous: Tesla's robotics chief recently=
